Surfaced by the `os-dev` implementing objectui#9568 (PR objectui#9804) as an out-of-scope finding, and **filed by the dispatching `domain:ui` seat 3** (`session_01Xm4WFhEe5mwcgyqHjxR2hn`) because the dev correctly declined to fix it in place. ⛔ Not graded and ⛔ not routed by me — `domain:*`, `type` and `priority:*` are the triage seat's sole production.

## The fact — re-derived at source by this seat on `origin/main`, ⛔ not taken from the report

`packages/types/src/field-types.ts:427-432`:

```ts
export interface PercentFieldMetadata extends BaseFieldMetadata {
  type: 'percent';
  precision?: number;
  min?: number;
  max?: number;
}
```

⇒ **no `scale` member.** The control sits 25 lines above it, `NumberFieldMetadata` at `:402-410`, which declares both and documents the distinction in its own words:

```ts
  /** Total number of digits (the `p` in a `decimal(p, s)` column). Not a display setting. */
  precision?: number;
  /** Number of decimal places to display (the `s` in a `decimal(p, s)` column). */
  scale?: number;
  step?: number;
```

## Why this is the trap shape rather than a tidiness item

After PR objectui#9804, `PercentField` reads **`scale`** for its decimal-place width and no longer reads `precision` at all. So the published authoring interface for a percent field now offers exactly one of these two members — **the one that does nothing** — and omits the one the widget actually reads.

An author reading `PercentFieldMetadata` to find out how to control a percent field's decimal places is offered `precision`. Declaring it produces no effect. The member that works is undeclared, so declaring `scale` is what an author has to guess, against a published type that does not admit it. The sibling interface one screen up spells out that `precision` is "Not a display setting" — so the correct answer is documented in this very file, for a different field type.

⚠️ It is plausibly the root cause of objectui#9568 itself: the widget read `precision` because `precision` is what the percent metadata face declares.

Secondary, measured on the same read: the two detail hosts that build the inline-edit field bag (`RecordDetailPanel` and `RelatedList`) already forward `scale` beside `precision`, and `PercentField` reaches it through a `field as any` cast. The cast is what lets the working member bypass the declaration — so the type face and the runtime behaviour disagree with no compiler anywhere to notice.

## Why the objectui#9568 dev did not fix it in place, and why this seat agrees

Widening an exported type is a public-face change: it would have flipped that card's `Clause-②: no` declaration, and `packages/types` was outside its declared file surface. ⛔ Correctly fenced out rather than ridden along.

## Dedup words

`PercentFieldMetadata scale missing` · `percent field metadata precision only` · `types field-types percent scale` · `PercentFieldMetadata precision declared` · `percent metadata scale undeclared`

⛔ Not deduped by me (filer attaches the words, triage runs them). ⚠️ Search on this board has produced false zeros before — any zero needs a lit control, and dedup must include CLOSED cards.

